explanation:
## How the puzzle works

**What the grids are made of.** Each input is mostly random noise in all ten colours. Somewhere in it sits one large solid rectangle of a single colour, and that colour is also the grid's most common one:
- pair 1: colour 4, rows 6–15 and columns 4–12;
- pair 2: colour 1, rows 5–11 and columns 8–13;
- pair 3: colour 8, rows 2–7 and columns 5–12.

Inside the rectangle are a few single "dot" cells of one other colour (1 in pair 1, 2 in pairs 2 and 3).

**How the output relates.** The output has exactly the rectangle's size: 10×9, 7×6 and 6×8. So the output is the rectangle cut out of the grid. Then every dot is extended into a full row and a full column of the dot colour.

For example, in pair 1 the dots sit at (8,6), (11,10) and (13,8). Relative to the rectangle's corner these are (2,2), (5,6) and (7,4). In the output:
- rows 2, 5 and 7 are entirely colour 1;
- columns 2, 6 and 4 are entirely colour 1.

Pairs 2 and 3 work the same way. In pair 3, the single dot at (5,8) becomes relative (3,3), giving one horizontal and one vertical line of 2.

**Locating the rectangle robustly.** The rectangle colour also appears scattered through the noise, and a few stray cells touch the rectangle's edge. In the test, (5,11) and (6,11) are 8 right next to the block, and so is (12,5). So I do not trust the raw bounding box of the biggest component. Instead I:
1. take the largest 4-connected component of the background colour;
2. take its bounding box;
3. repeatedly peel off any edge row or column where fewer than half the cells are that colour.

A stray protrusion contributes only one or two cells to its row or column, so it is peeled away. Every true edge line is nearly all rectangle colour, so it stays.

**The test input.** The rectangle is the 8-block at rows 2–11, columns 3–10, which is 10×8. Its dots are 1s at (4,9) and (7,5), which are (2,6) and (5,2) relative to the corner. So the output is a 10×8 grid of 8 with:
- rows 2 and 5 filled with 1;
- columns 6 and 2 filled with 1.

**Teaching stages:** first emit the cropped rectangle, then the canvas after each dot's cross is drawn, then the final canvas.
